    "content": "The ACT is a standardized college admissions test for high school students assessing English, math, reading, and science (plus optional writing) to measure college readiness. The test costs $68 (without writing), with a maximum score of 36 (19.4 average). Preparation involves studying academic standards, while any high school student can take it to show readiness. Key ACT Information Purpose: Measures high school academic development and college readiness, used for admissions and scholarships. Structure: Includes English, Math, Reading, and Science, with optional Writing. Total time ranges from 125 to 205 minutes depending on options. Passing Score: There is no \"passing\" score; it is a 1-36 scale. Average score is typically around 19–20. Eligibility/Requirements: Open to any student, usually high schoolers, with no specific prerequisites required. Cost (2025-2026): $68.00 (no writing), $93.00 (with writing). Preparation: Official prep is available at act.org, focusing on content mastery in English, Math, Reading, and Science. How to Prepare Use official ACT prep resources on their website. Review content areas (English, Math, Reading, Science). Take practice tests to get familiar with the timing and question types.",
